@page "/about"

<PageTitle>About — BlackHoleSim</PageTitle>

<h1>About BlackHoleSim</h1>
<p class="subtitle">
    Numerically integrates photon geodesics in <strong>Schwarzschild spacetime</strong>
    (G = c = M = 1) and renders a synthetic image of a black hole with a thin accretion disk.
</p>

<div class="card">
    <h2>Physics</h2>

    <h3>Schwarzschild metric (equatorial plane)</h3>
    <pre><code>ds² = -(1 - 2M/r) dt² + (1 - 2M/r)⁻¹ dr² + r² dφ²</code></pre>

    <h3>Null geodesics via Hamiltonian mechanics</h3>
    <p>Photon motion is derived from the Hamiltonian:</p>
    <pre><code>H = ½ g^μν p_μ p_ν = 0</code></pre>
    <p>Hamilton's equations give:</p>
    <pre><code>dq^μ/dλ =  ∂H/∂p_μ = g^μμ p_μ
dp_μ/dλ = -∂H/∂q^μ</code></pre>
    <p>
        Because <em>t</em> and <em>φ</em> are cyclic coordinates, their conjugate momenta
        <em>p<sub>t</sub></em> (energy) and <em>p<sub>φ</sub></em> (angular momentum) are conserved
        throughout the integration.
    </p>

    <h3>Integration</h3>
    <p>
        The classic 4th-order <strong>Runge–Kutta (RK4)</strong> integrator advances the
        6-component state vector (t, r, φ, p<sub>t</sub>, p<sub>r</sub>, p<sub>φ</sub>) along the
        affine parameter λ.
    </p>

    <h3>Key radii</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Event horizon:</strong> r = 2M — photons inside are captured</li>
        <li><strong>Photon sphere:</strong> r = 3M — unstable circular photon orbits</li>
        <li><strong>ISCO:</strong> r = 6M — inner edge of the accretion disk</li>
    </ul>
</div>

